{
  "id": "COMPOUND:1890 - PROTEIN:555",
  "verbalization": "Coenzyme A serves as a critical cofactor in multiple metabolic pathways, and its interaction with the Isocitrate dehydrogenase [NAD] subunit alpha, mitochondrial is mediated through a series of intermediate targets. The evidence suggests that Coenzyme A acts as a substrate for Acyl-CoA-binding protein, which in turn is targeted by (S)-Hmg-Coa and 1,4-Dithiothreitol. These molecules are known to regulate enzyme activity, and their interaction with the Isocitrate dehydrogenase subunit implies a regulatory role in the mitochondrial citric acid cycle. The presence of multiple pathways converging on this subunit underscores a potential mechanism where Coenzyme A, through its interaction with these intermediates, influences the activity of the enzyme. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role in metabolic regulation, with Coenzyme A serving as a central hub in the network of interactions leading to the activation or inhibition of the Isocitrate dehydrogenase subunit.",
